WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:03.899
[Music]

00:00:03.899 --> 00:00:06.766
An Airbus A330 is on a routine overnight

00:00:06.766 --> 00:00:08.533
flight from Rio de Janeiro to Paris.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.066
As it cruises over the vast darkness of

00:00:11.066 --> 00:00:13.233
the Atlantic Ocean at 35,000 feet,

00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:15.733
inside the cabin is quiet and peaceful.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.100
Many passengers are probably sleeping.

00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:20.233
All of a sudden, the autopilot

00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:23.066
disconnects and the aircraft begins rolling to the right.

00:00:23.066 --> 00:00:25.800
Within seconds, conflicting alarms start

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:26.666
blaring in the cockpit.

00:00:27.466 --> 00:00:29.300
Airspeed indications fluctuate wildly.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:30.800
"I have the controls,"

00:00:31.066 --> 00:00:31.866
calls the co-pilot.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:32.866
"But, what's happening?"

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:34.433
Less than four and a half minutes later,

00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:35.033
"we're going to crash"

00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:35.833
the aircraft falls

00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:37.033
nose-up into the ocean,

00:00:37.566 --> 00:00:38.500
at a vertical speed

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.299
exceeding 10,000 feet per minute.

00:00:41.266 --> 00:00:45.066
Tragically, all 228 souls are lost.

00:00:45.866 --> 00:00:48.200
For two agonizing years, the truth remained

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.333
locked inside flight recorders, almost

00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:52.333
13,000 feet below the Atlantic's surface.

00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:55.000
Grieving families bore the terrible pain

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.700
of not knowing exactly what happened in

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.000
their loved ones' final moments.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.399
When investigators finally recovered the black

00:01:01.399 --> 00:01:03.866
boxes, the devastating sequence of events

00:01:03.866 --> 00:01:04.966
they discovered would

00:01:04.966 --> 00:01:06.299
raise an unsettling question.

00:01:07.099 --> 00:01:08.433
Who was really flying the

00:01:08.433 --> 00:01:09.799
plane in those final few minutes?

00:01:11.266 --> 00:01:13.033
This is the story of

00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:14.533
Air France Flight 447.

00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:22.266
The media often refers to Air France 447

00:01:22.266 --> 00:01:24.266
as the "Titanic of the skies."

00:01:25.299 --> 00:01:27.500
It's an evocative metaphor, comparing two

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.566
advanced technological

00:01:28.566 --> 00:01:30.066
marvels from very different

00:01:30.466 --> 00:01:31.866
times, both of which met

00:01:31.866 --> 00:01:33.566
tragic ends that shocked the world.

00:01:34.166 --> 00:01:36.733
For almost two years, a haunting question

00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:37.700
remained unanswered.

00:01:38.366 --> 00:01:39.566
How could one of the world's most

00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:40.799
sophisticated passenger

00:01:40.799 --> 00:01:42.966
aircraft simply 'fall from the sky'?

00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:45.966
This is a story of how human psychology

00:01:45.966 --> 00:01:47.733
can unexpectedly collide

00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:49.033
with advanced technology

00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:50.533
when met with a perfect

00:01:50.533 --> 00:01:52.166
storm of adverse conditions.

00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:58.866
May 31st, 2009, an Airbus A330-203 prepared

00:01:58.866 --> 00:01:59.733
for departure from

00:01:59.733 --> 00:02:01.533
Rio de Janeiro Galeão Airport.

00:02:02.566 --> 00:02:04.533
The aircraft, with registration 

00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:06.766
Foxtrot-Golf-Zulu-Charlie-Papa

00:02:06.766 --> 00:02:07.666
had been in service

00:02:07.666 --> 00:02:08.766
for just four years.

00:02:09.933 --> 00:02:11.500
Routine maintenance had been performed

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.033
according to schedule, and

00:02:13.033 --> 00:02:14.533
the aircraft had no significant

00:02:14.566 --> 00:02:16.833
technical issues beyond a minor problem

00:02:16.833 --> 00:02:18.166
with one of the radio panels.

00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:20.633
The flight crew consisted

00:02:20.633 --> 00:02:22.233
of three experienced pilots.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:25.966
Captain Marc Dubois, aged 58, had logged

00:02:25.966 --> 00:02:27.466
nearly 11,000 flight

00:02:27.466 --> 00:02:30.400
hours, including more than 1,700

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.433
on the A330.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.033
First Officer Pierre-Cédric Bonin, aged

00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:37.400
32, designated as Pilot

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.133
Flying for this leg, had

00:02:39.166 --> 00:02:41.666
accumulated almost 3,000 total flight

00:02:41.666 --> 00:02:44.333
hours, with 807 on type.

00:02:45.366 --> 00:02:47.733
His wife Isabelle, a physics teacher, was

00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:48.566
among the passengers

00:02:48.566 --> 00:02:49.533
aboard for this trip.

00:02:50.266 --> 00:02:52.033
Air France's procedures for long-haul

00:02:52.033 --> 00:02:53.199
flights required

00:02:53.199 --> 00:02:55.333
augmented crew to allow for rest

00:02:55.333 --> 00:02:57.066
periods during the 11-hour journey.

00:02:57.866 --> 00:02:59.333
The flight was scheduled to depart

00:02:59.333 --> 00:03:02.400
Rio de Janeiro Galeão airport at 10:00 p.m. local

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.733
time, arriving in Charles de Gaulle,

00:03:04.733 --> 00:03:05.800
Paris the following morning.

00:03:06.866 --> 00:03:08.266
This overnight flight meant the crew

00:03:08.266 --> 00:03:09.266
would be operating through

00:03:09.266 --> 00:03:10.766
what aviation physiologists

00:03:10.833 --> 00:03:13.099
call the "window of circadian low,"

00:03:13.833 --> 00:03:15.233
typically between 2:00-6:00 a.m.

00:03:15.233 --> 00:03:16.966
body clock time.

00:03:17.833 --> 00:03:19.733
During this period, the human brain

00:03:19.733 --> 00:03:21.900
experiences significant decreases in

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.566
alertness, decision-making

00:03:23.666 --> 00:03:26.900
ability, and situational awareness, even

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:28.199
in well-rested individuals.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.133
This physiological challenge is one key

00:03:31.133 --> 00:03:32.800
reason Air France, like

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.266
most carriers, schedules

00:03:34.466 --> 00:03:36.633
augmented crews for long-haul flights.

00:03:37.866 --> 00:03:40.000
The relief pilot, First Officer

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.266
David Robert, aged 37, had over

00:03:43.266 --> 00:03:45.466
6,500 flight hours in total.

00:03:46.199 --> 00:03:49.966
With almost 4,500 hours on the A330, he

00:03:49.966 --> 00:03:51.533
actually had more on-type

00:03:51.533 --> 00:03:53.099
experience than the captain

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:54.833
and other First Officer combined.

00:03:55.733 --> 00:03:57.666
Robert would be designated as Pilot Not

00:03:57.666 --> 00:03:59.566
Flying or Pilot Monitoring.

00:04:00.533 --> 00:04:02.199
He was primarily in a management position

00:04:02.199 --> 00:04:04.733
at Air France's operations center, flying

00:04:04.733 --> 00:04:06.766
occasionally to maintain his credentials.

00:04:08.733 --> 00:04:11.266
Loading was completed with 216 passengers

00:04:11.266 --> 00:04:12.933
and 12 crew members,

00:04:13.433 --> 00:04:14.800
including the 3 pilots.

00:04:15.866 --> 00:04:17.533
The passengers represented a global

00:04:17.533 --> 00:04:20.233
community from 32 different nations.

00:04:21.366 --> 00:04:22.933
French citizens constituted the largest

00:04:22.933 --> 00:04:25.366
group (61), followed

00:04:25.366 --> 00:04:27.866
closely by Brazilians (58) and

00:04:27.866 --> 00:04:28.933
Germans (26).

00:04:30.233 --> 00:04:32.033
Other nationalities included Chinese and

00:04:32.033 --> 00:04:34.866
Italian (9 each), Swiss (6),

00:04:35.266 --> 00:04:37.733
British (5), and two Americans.

00:04:38.733 --> 00:04:41.433
The aircraft carried 70.4 tons of fuel at

00:04:41.433 --> 00:04:42.766
takeoff, more than

00:04:42.766 --> 00:04:43.833
sufficient for the planned

00:04:43.833 --> 00:04:45.133
route with adequate

00:04:45.133 --> 00:04:46.533
reserves for contingencies.

00:04:47.899 --> 00:04:49.300
As the crew conducted pre-flight

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.833
preparations, they were

00:04:50.833 --> 00:04:52.133
aware that they would be crossing

00:04:52.133 --> 00:04:54.833
the intertropical convergence zone, an

00:04:54.833 --> 00:04:55.966
area where trade winds

00:04:55.966 --> 00:04:57.433
from the northern and southern

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.300
hemispheres meet, often creating bands of

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:00.833
significant

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:02.766
convective activity, including

00:05:02.766 --> 00:05:03.466
thunderstorms.

00:05:04.466 --> 00:05:05.533
This is part and parcel

00:05:05.533 --> 00:05:06.866
for flights along this route.

00:05:08.333 --> 00:05:09.633
Weather forecasts showed nothing

00:05:09.633 --> 00:05:11.233
exceptional for the time of year.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.233
SIGMET advisories indicated possible

00:05:14.233 --> 00:05:16.066
convective activity, but

00:05:16.066 --> 00:05:17.766
multiple daily flights navigated

00:05:17.766 --> 00:05:19.633
this same airspace without any issues.

00:05:20.666 --> 00:05:22.833
Satellite imagery later revealed a large

00:05:22.833 --> 00:05:24.766
cluster of storm cells along the route,

00:05:25.433 --> 00:05:26.566
powerful cumulonimbi,

00:05:26.966 --> 00:05:29.133
with tops reaching above the aircraft's

00:05:29.133 --> 00:05:30.699
cruising altitude, but

00:05:30.699 --> 00:05:31.600
these conditions were

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:33.433
within normal operational parameters.

00:05:34.766 --> 00:05:36.899
Following a delay of almost half an hour,

00:05:37.399 --> 00:05:38.266
the flight departed

00:05:38.266 --> 00:05:40.433
at 10:29 p.m. local time,

00:05:40.966 --> 00:05:41.899
climbing to its cruise

00:05:41.899 --> 00:05:43.966
altitude of 35,000 feet.

00:05:45.166 --> 00:05:47.066
Captain Dubois and First Officer Bonin

00:05:47.066 --> 00:05:48.733
managed the initial phase

00:05:48.733 --> 00:05:50.466
of the journey, while First

00:05:50.466 --> 00:05:52.433
Officer Robert rested in the crew rest

00:05:52.433 --> 00:05:54.266
compartment beside the cockpit.

00:05:54.866 --> 00:05:55.866
All good so far.

00:05:56.833 --> 00:05:57.800
The night ahead looked like

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.433
any routine oceanic crossing.

00:06:01.866 --> 00:06:03.933
There's no radar coverage in most oceanic

00:06:03.933 --> 00:06:05.333
regions, meaning air

00:06:05.333 --> 00:06:06.566
traffic controllers can't

00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:08.133
"see" aircraft on their screens.

00:06:09.033 --> 00:06:10.966
Instead, planes follow predetermined

00:06:10.966 --> 00:06:12.166
tracks and report their

00:06:12.166 --> 00:06:13.966
positions at specified points.

00:06:14.833 --> 00:06:19.133
At 01:35, Air France 447 reaches the

00:06:19.133 --> 00:06:20.600
INTOL waypoint, marking

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.033
the transition from Brazilian

00:06:22.033 --> 00:06:24.333
radar coverage to oceanic airspace

00:06:24.333 --> 00:06:26.333
controlled by the ATLANTICO center.

00:06:27.199 --> 00:06:29.566
The crew makes radio contact, reporting

00:06:29.566 --> 00:06:30.766
their position and

00:06:30.766 --> 00:06:32.199
providing estimates for upcoming

00:06:32.199 --> 00:06:32.766
waypoints.

00:06:33.466 --> 00:06:34.966
They attempt to establish what's known as

00:06:34.966 --> 00:06:37.033
an ADS-C data connection

00:06:37.033 --> 00:06:39.000
with the DAKAR Oceanic

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.633
center but are unsuccessful.

00:06:41.533 --> 00:06:43.466
This satellite-based position reporting

00:06:43.466 --> 00:06:45.199
system would have automatically

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:46.666
transmitted the aircraft's

00:06:46.666 --> 00:06:48.533
position to air traffic control.

00:06:50.399 --> 00:06:51.766
"So we've got a thing straight ahead,"

00:06:51.899 --> 00:06:54.066
Bonin remarks, referring to some weather

00:06:54.066 --> 00:06:55.699
in their path, adjusting

00:06:55.699 --> 00:06:56.966
his navigation display.

00:06:57.933 --> 00:07:00.166
Captain Dubois acknowledges, and the two

00:07:00.166 --> 00:07:01.500
discuss the outside air

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:02.966
temperature, which is running

00:07:02.966 --> 00:07:04.133
higher than forecast.

00:07:04.766 --> 00:07:07.000
This means, in practice, that the

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:08.033
aircraft would be unable

00:07:08.033 --> 00:07:09.399
to climb up to Flight Level

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:12.666
370, which might have provided a smoother

00:07:12.666 --> 00:07:14.733
ride above the developing weather.

00:07:16.033 --> 00:07:17.199
Aircraft have a ceiling of

00:07:17.199 --> 00:07:18.633
how high they can safely fly.

00:07:19.899 --> 00:07:21.899
This is determined by how thick or thin

00:07:21.899 --> 00:07:23.566
the air is, and because

00:07:23.566 --> 00:07:24.833
warmer air is less dense

00:07:24.833 --> 00:07:26.666
with the air molecules further apart,

00:07:27.366 --> 00:07:28.033
this essentially

00:07:28.033 --> 00:07:29.933
translates into less lift available

00:07:30.066 --> 00:07:31.199
for the aircraft's wings.

00:07:32.833 --> 00:07:36.233
Ten minutes later, at 01:45, the

00:07:36.233 --> 00:07:37.833
aircraft entered a zone of light

00:07:37.833 --> 00:07:39.333
turbulence approaching

00:07:39.333 --> 00:07:40.466
the SALPU waypoint.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:44.066
The crew dims the cockpit lighting and

00:07:44.066 --> 00:07:45.066
turns on the external

00:07:45.066 --> 00:07:46.466
lights to improve visibility.

00:07:48.433 --> 00:07:50.100
"We're entering the cloud layer," 

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:51.766
Bonin notes, adding that "it

00:07:51.766 --> 00:07:52.733
would have been good to be

00:07:52.733 --> 00:07:53.399
able to climb."

00:07:54.233 --> 00:07:55.899
The turbulence continues for several

00:07:55.899 --> 00:07:57.166
minutes before subsiding.

00:07:57.833 --> 00:07:59.633
The higher-than-expected temperature has

00:07:59.633 --> 00:08:00.533
pushed their maximum

00:08:00.533 --> 00:08:02.333
recommended altitude, known

00:08:02.333 --> 00:08:07.566
as "REC MAX," to Flight Level 375 or 37,500 ft

00:08:07.566 --> 00:08:09.066
just out of practical reach.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.766
"It's going to be turbulent when I go for

00:08:11.766 --> 00:08:13.966
my rest," Captain Dubois said, his words

00:08:14.066 --> 00:08:15.133
heavy with an irony

00:08:15.133 --> 00:08:16.533
only fate could comprehend.

00:08:17.466 --> 00:08:19.633
He wakes First Officer Robert, who has

00:08:19.633 --> 00:08:20.433
been sleeping in the

00:08:20.433 --> 00:08:21.366
crew rest compartment.

00:08:22.166 --> 00:08:24.133
"He's going to take my place," Dubois

00:08:24.133 --> 00:08:25.866
tells Bonin, referring to

00:08:25.866 --> 00:08:27.533
Robert, who has just entered

00:08:27.533 --> 00:08:28.366
the cockpit and will

00:08:28.366 --> 00:08:29.733
now occupy the left seat.

00:08:31.266 --> 00:08:31.966
This was the beginning

00:08:31.966 --> 00:08:33.566
of a subtle power dynamic.

00:08:34.166 --> 00:08:36.833
The less experienced pilot, Bonin, was

00:08:36.833 --> 00:08:38.133
designated as Pilot

00:08:38.133 --> 00:08:40.366
Flying, while the more experienced

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:43.333
and management-level co-pilot, Robert,

00:08:43.333 --> 00:08:44.600
took the monitoring role

00:08:44.633 --> 00:08:46.299
otherwise known as "Pilot

00:08:46.299 --> 00:08:47.566
Not Flying."

00:08:48.899 --> 00:08:50.366
It's approximately 02:00 a.m.

00:08:51.366 --> 00:08:53.033
With Captain Dubois about to leave the

00:08:53.033 --> 00:08:54.233
flight deck, First

00:08:54.233 --> 00:08:56.133
Officer Bonin briefs Robert.

00:08:56.166 --> 00:08:57.799
"Well, the little bit of turbulence that

00:08:57.799 --> 00:08:58.833
you just saw, we

00:08:58.833 --> 00:09:00.266
should find the same ahead.

00:09:00.266 --> 00:09:01.233
We're in the cloud layer.

00:09:01.666 --> 00:09:03.333
Unfortunately, we can't climb much for

00:09:03.333 --> 00:09:04.500
the moment because the

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:05.366
temperature is falling

00:09:05.366 --> 00:09:06.566
more slowly than forecast.

00:09:07.366 --> 00:09:09.166
So what we have is some REC MAX a

00:09:09.166 --> 00:09:10.233
little too low to get

00:09:10.233 --> 00:09:11.766
to three seven," explains Bonin.

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:14.700
So in other words, the First Officer was

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:16.166
explaining that they expect more

00:09:16.166 --> 00:09:17.600
turbulence ahead and

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:19.666
the warm, thinner air is making it unsafe

00:09:19.666 --> 00:09:21.733
to climb to 37,000 feet

00:09:21.733 --> 00:09:23.133
to escape the weather.

00:09:24.166 --> 00:09:25.333
He also mentions the

00:09:25.333 --> 00:09:26.700
failed logon with DAKAR.

00:09:27.633 --> 00:09:29.333
Remember, this meant the aircraft

00:09:29.333 --> 00:09:30.833
couldn't establish the satellite

00:09:30.833 --> 00:09:32.200
connection that would

00:09:32.233 --> 00:09:34.366
automatically report its position to air

00:09:34.366 --> 00:09:35.333
traffic controllers,

00:09:36.166 --> 00:09:36.966
essentially making the

00:09:36.966 --> 00:09:37.833
plane even less

00:09:37.833 --> 00:09:39.366
visible over the open ocean.

00:09:40.666 --> 00:09:42.600
The captain departs for his rest period,

00:09:43.133 --> 00:09:44.933
leaving the two co-pilots in control.

00:09:46.333 --> 00:09:47.500
Bonin had expressed concerns

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:49.033
even before the captain left.

00:09:49.966 --> 00:09:51.433
He had mentioned the turbulence several

00:09:51.433 --> 00:09:52.666
times and suggested

00:09:52.666 --> 00:09:55.266
climbing to flight level 360

00:09:55.533 --> 00:09:57.966
to avoid the cloud layer, saying, "it

00:09:57.966 --> 00:09:58.799
would have been good

00:09:58.799 --> 00:10:00.066
to be able to climb."

00:10:00.766 --> 00:10:02.533
We've no way of knowing exactly what was

00:10:02.533 --> 00:10:03.933
going on inside Bonin's

00:10:03.933 --> 00:10:05.700
head, but it is reasonable

00:10:05.766 --> 00:10:08.333
to assume that his preoccupation with the

00:10:08.333 --> 00:10:09.166
weather situation

00:10:09.166 --> 00:10:11.266
revealed a level of anxiety

00:10:11.833 --> 00:10:13.733
that may have been building in his mind.

00:10:15.133 --> 00:10:17.433
The handover between Captain Dubois and

00:10:17.433 --> 00:10:18.766
Relief Pilot First Officer

00:10:18.766 --> 00:10:20.966
Robert is a critical moment

00:10:20.966 --> 00:10:22.033
in this flight.

00:10:22.766 --> 00:10:25.000
Good Crew Resource Management (CRM)

00:10:25.366 --> 00:10:26.666
requires crystal clear

00:10:26.666 --> 00:10:28.666
communication, and it seems

00:10:28.766 --> 00:10:30.899
that the captain never explicitly said

00:10:30.899 --> 00:10:33.133
who would be in command in his absence.

00:10:33.866 --> 00:10:35.933
Also, the captain didn't leave any

00:10:35.933 --> 00:10:37.566
specific instructions on

00:10:37.566 --> 00:10:38.666
how they should cross the

00:10:38.666 --> 00:10:40.299
Intertropical Convergence Zone.

00:10:42.166 --> 00:10:43.766
The aircraft approaches the ORARO

00:10:43.766 --> 00:10:45.333
waypoint, maintaining

00:10:45.333 --> 00:10:48.799
Flight Level 350 or 35,000 feet,

00:10:49.266 --> 00:10:51.566
at a cruising speed of Mach 0.82.

00:10:52.666 --> 00:10:54.533
The two co-pilots discuss the temperature

00:10:54.533 --> 00:10:56.233
and "REC MAX" again,

00:10:56.733 --> 00:10:58.133
noting its gradual increase.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:00.866
The turbulence intensifies slightly.

00:11:02.433 --> 00:11:05.000
At 02:06, Bonin lets the cabin crew

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:05.866
know that they're going

00:11:05.866 --> 00:11:06.866
to be shortly entering

00:11:06.933 --> 00:11:07.666
some turbulence.

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:11.533
Approximately two minutes later, at 02:08,

00:11:11.533 --> 00:11:13.333
Robert suggests,

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:14.633
"Go to the left a bit..."

00:11:15.466 --> 00:11:17.566
The heading mode is activated, and they

00:11:17.566 --> 00:11:18.766
change course by about

00:11:18.766 --> 00:11:20.866
12 degrees, deviating from

00:11:20.899 --> 00:11:22.933
their planned route to avoid weather that

00:11:22.933 --> 00:11:24.299
was detected on their radar.

00:11:26.333 --> 00:11:27.966
Inside the cockpit, the

00:11:27.966 --> 00:11:29.600
smell of ozone became noticeable.

00:11:30.533 --> 00:11:32.133
According to the investigation's final

00:11:32.133 --> 00:11:33.233
report for this flight,

00:11:34.033 --> 00:11:35.333
Bonin didn't seem to recognize

00:11:35.333 --> 00:11:37.633
this environmental cue as they entered

00:11:37.633 --> 00:11:39.633
this meteorologically active area.

00:11:40.033 --> 00:11:41.766
"What's that smell, now?" he asked.

00:11:42.166 --> 00:11:43.466
"It's... it's ozone," replied the more

00:11:43.466 --> 00:11:45.333
experienced Robert from the left seat.

00:11:46.766 --> 00:11:48.933
Ozone is a distinctive electric scent

00:11:48.933 --> 00:11:49.933
often present during

00:11:49.933 --> 00:11:51.833
thunderstorms—a natural

00:11:51.833 --> 00:11:53.600
warning sign that they were entering an

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:54.633
active weather system.

00:11:56.233 --> 00:11:58.033
Robert adjusts the weather radar's gain

00:11:58.033 --> 00:11:59.566
up to maximum. The crew

00:11:59.566 --> 00:12:00.866
decides to reduce speed

00:12:00.899 --> 00:12:03.333
slightly to about Mach 0.8, and they

00:12:03.333 --> 00:12:05.833
activate the engine anti-icing systems.

00:12:07.233 --> 00:12:09.433
These routine decisions – subtle course

00:12:09.433 --> 00:12:10.933
changes, minor speed

00:12:10.933 --> 00:12:12.933
adjustments, system activations –

00:12:13.666 --> 00:12:15.600
are all standard practice carried out by

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:16.566
a professional crew

00:12:16.566 --> 00:12:17.966
when navigating through

00:12:18.033 --> 00:12:19.433
areas of convective weather.

00:12:20.233 --> 00:12:22.266
By now, the sound of ice crystals

00:12:22.266 --> 00:12:23.333
impacting the aircraft

00:12:23.333 --> 00:12:25.700
could be heard, another telltale

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:27.233
sign of being in the middle

00:12:27.233 --> 00:12:28.466
of an active thunderstorm.

00:12:29.533 --> 00:12:31.700
Little did the pilots know, what lay

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:32.799
ahead of them wasn't

00:12:32.799 --> 00:12:33.833
just a weather system.

00:12:34.433 --> 00:12:35.966
They were unknowingly approaching

00:12:35.966 --> 00:12:37.933
turbulence of a very different nature:

00:12:38.733 --> 00:12:40.066
the complex interplay of weather

00:12:40.066 --> 00:12:42.833
conditions, human psychology, and

00:12:42.833 --> 00:12:43.933
automated technology

00:12:43.933 --> 00:12:45.933
that was designed to keep them safe

00:12:45.933 --> 00:12:48.333
but would soon become part of the problem.

00:12:49.533 --> 00:12:52.633
Suddenly, at exactly 02:10:06 am,

00:12:53.166 --> 00:12:53.799
the autopilot

00:12:53.799 --> 00:12:55.166
disconnects with a distinctive

00:12:55.266 --> 00:12:56.433
cavalry charge alarm.

00:12:57.133 --> 00:12:59.166
The aircraft begins rolling to the right.

00:12:59.166 --> 00:13:00.233
"I have the controls,"

00:13:00.233 --> 00:13:01.433
calls First Officer Bonin

00:13:01.433 --> 00:13:02.266
from the right seat.

00:13:03.066 --> 00:13:05.733
Almost immediately, a stall warning – a

00:13:05.733 --> 00:13:06.766
synthetic voice calling

00:13:06.766 --> 00:13:08.766
"Stall, stall" – triggers briefly,

00:13:09.033 --> 00:13:09.833
twice in succession.

00:13:11.066 --> 00:13:13.233
Within 10 seconds, Bonin's nose-up input

00:13:13.233 --> 00:13:14.433
increases the aircraft's

00:13:14.433 --> 00:13:15.866
pitch attitude from 2 degrees

00:13:15.866 --> 00:13:16.833
to 11 degrees.

00:13:17.733 --> 00:13:19.466
The aircraft begins to climb rapidly,

00:13:19.933 --> 00:13:21.366
despite the engine thrust being

00:13:21.366 --> 00:13:22.333
insufficient for such

00:13:22.333 --> 00:13:23.666
a maneuver at this altitude.

00:13:24.566 --> 00:13:26.433
The consequence is immediate and severe.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:28.966
The speed indications on the left primary

00:13:28.966 --> 00:13:30.866
flight display (PFD)

00:13:31.633 --> 00:13:32.500
plummet from approximately

00:13:32.766 --> 00:13:36.066
275 knots to 60 knots in seconds.

00:13:37.033 --> 00:13:39.266
Shortly afterward, the same occurs on the

00:13:39.266 --> 00:13:40.733
standby airspeed indicator.

00:13:41.733 --> 00:13:43.533
Robert asks, "What is that?" in response

00:13:43.533 --> 00:13:44.700
to the various warnings.

00:13:45.366 --> 00:13:46.633
Bonin replies, "We haven't

00:13:46.633 --> 00:13:50.266
got a good... display of speed."

00:13:51.233 --> 00:13:53.033
As he was saying this, First Officer

00:13:53.033 --> 00:13:54.533
Robert called out, "We've

00:13:54.533 --> 00:13:55.566
lost the speeds," followed

00:13:55.566 --> 00:13:57.133
by "alternate law protections,"

00:13:57.733 --> 00:13:59.100
recognizing the change in

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:00.466
Airbus flight control laws.

00:14:02.066 --> 00:14:04.066
Before we continue, let's pause to

00:14:04.066 --> 00:14:04.799
understand what's

00:14:04.799 --> 00:14:06.333
happening to Flight 447.

00:14:09.166 --> 00:14:12.233
The Airbus A330 has three separate pitot

00:14:12.233 --> 00:14:13.333
tubes that measure

00:14:13.333 --> 00:14:15.066
airspeed via three independent

00:14:15.233 --> 00:14:16.233
Air Data Computers.

00:14:17.399 --> 00:14:19.733
These pitot tubes are small, cylindrical

00:14:19.733 --> 00:14:21.166
probes that stick out

00:14:21.166 --> 00:14:22.633
from the aircraft's fuselage

00:14:23.166 --> 00:14:23.833
near the nose.

00:14:25.333 --> 00:14:27.266
They have a forward-facing hole that

00:14:27.266 --> 00:14:28.866
allows oncoming air to enter.

00:14:29.799 --> 00:14:32.233
As the aircraft moves forward, air is "rammed"

00:14:32.233 --> 00:14:34.433
into this tube, creating pressure that

00:14:34.433 --> 00:14:35.566
sensors can measure.

00:14:36.933 --> 00:14:38.633
This pressure is compared with static air

00:14:38.633 --> 00:14:40.000
pressure to calculate

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:41.133
the aircraft's airspeed.

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:44.733
This redundancy of having three separate pitot

00:14:44.733 --> 00:14:47.066
tubes linked to three separate computers

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:49.233
is designed to protect against

00:14:49.233 --> 00:14:50.899
any single component failure.

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:54.133
However, if all three pitot tubes

00:14:54.133 --> 00:14:55.666
encounter the same environmental

00:14:55.666 --> 00:14:57.433
conditions at the same time—

00:14:58.033 --> 00:15:00.466
like what Air France 447 was experiencing

00:15:00.466 --> 00:15:01.466
at that very moment—

00:15:01.899 --> 00:15:03.200
they can all fail together.

00:15:04.966 --> 00:15:06.799
Flying through the violent thunderstorm cell

00:15:06.799 --> 00:15:09.066
with its dense concentration of ice

00:15:09.066 --> 00:15:12.299
crystals known as graupel, the pitot

00:15:12.299 --> 00:15:13.166
tubes have become

00:15:13.166 --> 00:15:14.799
blocked by this graupel.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:17.700
Despite the pitot tubes' heating systems,

00:15:18.366 --> 00:15:20.466
the ice is accumulating faster than it

00:15:20.466 --> 00:15:22.866
can melt and drain in these particular

00:15:22.866 --> 00:15:24.566
atmospheric conditions.

00:15:25.466 --> 00:15:27.633
Such blockages typically clear within one

00:15:27.633 --> 00:15:30.166
to two minutes as heating elements work—

00:15:31.066 --> 00:15:33.600
a temporary situation pilots are trained

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:36.233
to manage using established procedures.

00:15:37.666 --> 00:15:39.899
As these blockages disrupt the pressure

00:15:39.899 --> 00:15:42.299
readings, each system begins reporting

00:15:42.299 --> 00:15:45.100
different, fluctuating airspeed values—

00:15:45.899 --> 00:15:47.899
causing the aircraft's computers to

00:15:47.899 --> 00:15:49.500
receive contradictory information.

00:15:51.366 --> 00:15:52.766
When two or more of these systems provide

00:15:52.766 --> 00:15:54.433
conflicting data, the

00:15:54.433 --> 00:15:55.899
aircraft triggers an "unreliable

00:15:56.033 --> 00:15:58.533
airspeed indication" - exactly what First

00:15:58.533 --> 00:15:59.700
Officer Robert meant

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:01.233
when he reported, "We've

00:16:01.233 --> 00:16:01.933
lost the speeds."

00:16:03.233 --> 00:16:04.600
Unknown to the passengers sleeping

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:05.666
peacefully in the cabin,

00:16:06.566 --> 00:16:08.299
Air France had recently begun

00:16:08.299 --> 00:16:11.033
upgrading to newer, more robust pitot

00:16:11.033 --> 00:16:12.433
probes across their fleet,

00:16:13.033 --> 00:16:14.500
following reports of similar

00:16:14.533 --> 00:16:15.399
icing incidents.

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:18.600
For now though, this aircraft continues

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:19.600
its journey through the

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:21.433
night sky with the original,

00:16:21.899 --> 00:16:22.933
less efficient pitot

00:16:22.933 --> 00:16:24.466
tubes still in place.

00:16:25.866 --> 00:16:28.033
This inconsistent data triggers a

00:16:28.033 --> 00:16:29.200
critical change in the

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:30.799
Airbus's flight control system.

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:34.399
The system switches from "Normal Law" to

00:16:34.399 --> 00:16:36.799
"Alternate Law," fundamentally altering

00:16:36.866 --> 00:16:37.700
how the aircraft

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:39.133
responds to pilot inputs.

00:16:40.233 --> 00:16:43.233
In Normal Law, Airbus's fly-by-wire system

00:16:43.233 --> 00:16:44.200
acts as a safety

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:45.866
measure, preventing pilots

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:47.966
from exceeding safe operating parameters,

00:16:48.433 --> 00:16:50.700
for example by being too aggressive with

00:16:50.700 --> 00:16:51.666
their control inputs.

00:16:52.633 --> 00:16:54.600
Now in Alternate Law, several automatic

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:55.933
protections are disabled.

00:16:56.933 --> 00:16:59.200
Most crucially, stall protection is no

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:00.899
longer available, meaning the

00:17:00.899 --> 00:17:02.100
aircraft won't automatically

00:17:02.233 --> 00:17:03.966
prevent dangerous pitch up movements.

00:17:04.833 --> 00:17:06.700
The aircraft is now in a less automated,

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:08.200
more manual mode,

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:09.733
meaning that the pilot flying

00:17:09.733 --> 00:17:11.233
needs to be very careful with his

00:17:11.233 --> 00:17:12.466
movements of the sidestick.

00:17:13.766 --> 00:17:15.900
One last important point to mention here

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:18.333
is that Airbus's separate sidesticks

00:17:18.333 --> 00:17:20.566
don't move in unison like

00:17:20.566 --> 00:17:22.433
Boeing's linked control columns,

00:17:23.166 --> 00:17:25.133
meaning the pilots can't physically feel

00:17:25.133 --> 00:17:26.466
each other's control inputs—

00:17:27.433 --> 00:17:29.400
and this fact is about to become a

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:31.333
monumental communication challenge for

00:17:31.333 --> 00:17:33.533
the flight crew of Flight 447.

00:17:34.799 --> 00:17:37.833
Not only that, but if both pilots make inputs

00:17:37.833 --> 00:17:38.733
on their sidesticks

00:17:38.733 --> 00:17:40.466
simultaneously, something

00:17:40.466 --> 00:17:41.700
unexpected happens—

00:17:42.266 --> 00:17:43.933
the inputs get averaged out.

00:17:44.733 --> 00:17:47.366
So, for example, if one pilot pulls back

00:17:47.366 --> 00:17:48.700
on the sidestick and

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:49.700
the other pushes forward

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:52.700
to the same extent, the net effect is

00:17:52.700 --> 00:17:53.633
that the inputs cancel

00:17:53.633 --> 00:17:55.333
each other out and nothing

00:17:55.333 --> 00:17:56.166
would happen.

00:17:57.133 --> 00:17:59.000
Still only mere seconds after the

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:00.166
autopilot disconnected,

00:18:01.133 --> 00:18:02.400
Robert is trying to work through

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:04.700
the aircraft's automated messages to

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:05.900
figure out what's happening.

00:18:07.333 --> 00:18:09.299
Adding to the confusion, the flight

00:18:09.299 --> 00:18:11.400
directors, providing visual

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:12.866
cues for pitch and roll on

00:18:12.866 --> 00:18:14.966
the pilots' displays, initially

00:18:14.966 --> 00:18:17.333
disappeared when the autopilot

00:18:17.333 --> 00:18:19.166
disconnected, but then

00:18:19.166 --> 00:18:20.633
reappeared shortly after.

00:18:21.566 --> 00:18:23.700
They began displaying guidance based on

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:24.833
incorrect information,

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:26.866
potentially suggesting pitch-up

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:29.033
commands at the very moment when the

00:18:29.033 --> 00:18:30.033
proper response would

00:18:30.033 --> 00:18:32.000
have been to pitch down.

00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:34.433
Robert says, "Wait, we're losing,"

00:18:34.733 --> 00:18:36.733
followed by "wing anti-ice"

00:18:37.099 --> 00:18:37.900
suggesting concern

00:18:37.900 --> 00:18:39.066
about icing conditions.

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:42.400
While Bonin is still making pitch

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:44.599
adjustments, Robert is concerned about

00:18:44.599 --> 00:18:45.433
their speed dropping

00:18:45.433 --> 00:18:47.900
as a result of the climb and calls out,

00:18:47.900 --> 00:18:49.000
"Watch your speed, watch

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:50.366
your speed." Bonin responds,

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:52.799
"Okay, okay, okay, I'm going back down."

00:18:53.833 --> 00:18:54.433
"Stabilize,"

00:18:54.799 --> 00:18:57.200
Robert instructs, followed immediately by

00:18:57.200 --> 00:18:58.633
an acknowledgement from Bonin:

00:18:58.633 --> 00:18:59.000
"Yeah."

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:01.833
Robert's concern about their climbing

00:19:01.833 --> 00:19:03.900
trend continues, "Go back down."

00:19:04.633 --> 00:19:06.299
Bonin's instrument panel is showing the

00:19:06.299 --> 00:19:07.366
aircraft climbing above

00:19:07.366 --> 00:19:08.933
their assigned cruising altitude.

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:11.666
Robert: "According to that, we're going

00:19:11.666 --> 00:19:13.099
up," adding with urgency,

00:19:13.366 --> 00:19:14.400
"According to all three,

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:16.599
you're going up, so go back down, okay."

00:19:17.299 --> 00:19:19.533
Bonin acknowledges, but unfortunately his

00:19:19.533 --> 00:19:20.833
sidestick inputs don't

00:19:20.833 --> 00:19:22.099
match his verbal confirmation.

00:19:22.566 --> 00:19:23.066
"You're at...

00:19:23.333 --> 00:19:24.200
Go back down,"

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:25.400
Robert repeats, watching

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:26.733
their altitude increase.

00:19:26.933 --> 00:19:28.533
"It's going, we're going back down,"

00:19:29.266 --> 00:19:30.933
though the aircraft's trajectory doesn't

00:19:30.933 --> 00:19:32.733
change substantially. "Gently,"

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:35.733
Robert cautions, possibly noting Bonin's

00:19:35.733 --> 00:19:36.966
aggressive control inputs.

00:19:37.599 --> 00:19:39.466
Robert offers, "I'll put you in ATT,"

00:19:39.766 --> 00:19:41.700
most likely referring to attitude mode,

00:19:42.099 --> 00:19:43.099
believing it might help

00:19:43.099 --> 00:19:44.333
stabilize the aircraft.

00:19:44.733 --> 00:19:45.533
"So what's that?"

00:19:45.633 --> 00:19:48.166
Robert asks, showing his confusion about

00:19:48.166 --> 00:19:49.200
the aircraft's behavior.

00:19:50.099 --> 00:19:51.200
Bonin confirms, "We're

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:52.466
in, yeah, we're in climb,"

00:19:53.133 --> 00:19:54.266
as they continue ascending

00:19:54.266 --> 00:19:56.000
past their assigned altitude.

00:19:57.466 --> 00:19:59.799
At this point, a mere 46 seconds have

00:19:59.799 --> 00:20:00.866
elapsed since the first

00:20:00.866 --> 00:20:02.633
sign of trouble began when

00:20:02.633 --> 00:20:03.900
the autopilot disconnected.

00:20:05.233 --> 00:20:06.933
First Officer Robert calls for the

00:20:06.933 --> 00:20:08.133
captain by pressing a call

00:20:08.133 --> 00:20:09.266
button that sounds in the

00:20:09.266 --> 00:20:09.866
crew restroom.

00:20:11.066 --> 00:20:11.733
"Where is he?"

00:20:11.733 --> 00:20:13.966
he utters, exclaiming it with urgency,

00:20:14.200 --> 00:20:15.233
showing signs of stress.

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:18.633
Robert begins calling for Captain Dubois

00:20:18.633 --> 00:20:19.966
repeatedly, asking,

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:21.400
"Is he coming or not?"

00:20:23.099 --> 00:20:25.200
The situation now escalates dramatically.

00:20:25.833 --> 00:20:27.799
After briefly seeming to stabilize with

00:20:27.799 --> 00:20:29.033
pitch around 6 degrees

00:20:29.033 --> 00:20:30.866
and angle of attack below

00:20:30.966 --> 00:20:32.533
5 degrees, the stall

00:20:32.533 --> 00:20:33.666
warning triggers again.

00:20:34.933 --> 00:20:36.700
This time, it sounds continuously,

00:20:37.099 --> 00:20:38.833
accompanied by the cricket sound

00:20:38.833 --> 00:20:40.000
specifically designed

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:41.000
to grab attention.

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:44.299
Meanwhile in the cabin, passengers and

00:20:44.299 --> 00:20:45.166
crew were most likely

00:20:45.166 --> 00:20:46.633
beginning to sense something

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:47.400
wasn't right.

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:50.633
A female voice is heard saying "hello" a

00:20:50.633 --> 00:20:51.533
couple of times on the

00:20:51.533 --> 00:20:53.366
interphone, indicating cabin

00:20:53.366 --> 00:20:54.466
crew are becoming aware

00:20:54.466 --> 00:20:55.833
of the abnormal situation.

00:20:56.766 --> 00:20:58.333
Who knows what thoughts were racing

00:20:58.333 --> 00:20:59.533
through their minds as they

00:20:59.533 --> 00:21:01.200
felt the erratic movements

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:02.233
of the aircraft.

00:21:03.233 --> 00:21:05.733
Back in the cockpit, vibration noises can

00:21:05.733 --> 00:21:06.633
now be heard as the

00:21:06.633 --> 00:21:08.099
airframe begins to buffet

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:09.566
due to disturbed airflow.

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:11.766
At this point, we're still only

00:21:11.766 --> 00:21:13.900
approximately 60 seconds into the ordeal.

00:21:14.933 --> 00:21:17.266
Robert urges "above all, try to touch the

00:21:17.266 --> 00:21:19.366
lateral controls as little as possible eh"

00:21:20.133 --> 00:21:21.900
This is proper procedure for unreliable

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:23.833
airspeed: minimize roll

00:21:23.833 --> 00:21:25.400
inputs to maintain stability.

00:21:26.299 --> 00:21:28.900
The stall warning persists again, "Stall,

00:21:29.266 --> 00:21:30.933
stall," with Bonin responding

00:21:30.933 --> 00:21:33.033
"I'm in TOGA, eh," indicating

00:21:33.233 --> 00:21:34.500
he believes maximum power

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:35.866
should resolve their situation.

00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:39.099
This contradictory response, applying

00:21:39.099 --> 00:21:41.500
power while pitching up, reveals a

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:42.933
fundamental confusion

00:21:42.933 --> 00:21:44.700
about the aircraft's energy state.

00:21:45.433 --> 00:21:46.333
He should have lowered the

00:21:46.333 --> 00:21:48.000
nose instead of raising it.

00:21:49.466 --> 00:21:51.299
During this time, the trimmable

00:21:51.299 --> 00:21:53.033
horizontal stabilizer begins

00:21:53.033 --> 00:21:55.033
to move, gradually shifting

00:21:55.099 --> 00:21:58.099
from 3 degrees nose up to an eventual 13

00:21:58.099 --> 00:21:58.933
degrees nose up

00:21:58.933 --> 00:22:00.599
position over the next minute.

00:22:01.799 --> 00:22:03.500
This trim movement significantly

00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:05.533
compounds the difficulty of recovery.

00:22:06.833 --> 00:22:09.500
As the aircraft continues to climb, the

00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:10.566
angle of attack continues

00:22:10.566 --> 00:22:12.799
increasing as Bonin maintains

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:14.099
his nose up inputs.

00:22:14.900 --> 00:22:17.233
The roll oscillations begin to develop as

00:22:17.233 --> 00:22:19.400
the aircraft reaches its maximum altitude

00:22:19.766 --> 00:22:21.266
of 38,000 feet.

00:22:22.266 --> 00:22:24.000
The pitch attitude and angle of attack

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:25.400
both reach 16 degrees,

00:22:26.066 --> 00:22:26.966
well beyond the critical

00:22:26.966 --> 00:22:27.900
angle for sustained

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:29.233
flight at this altitude.

00:22:30.666 --> 00:22:32.799
The angle of attack, which is the angle

00:22:32.799 --> 00:22:33.799
between the wing and the

00:22:33.799 --> 00:22:35.500
oncoming airflow, had been

00:22:35.500 --> 00:22:37.566
gradually increasing when the stall

00:22:37.566 --> 00:22:38.766
warning sounds again.

00:22:39.533 --> 00:22:41.599
As angle of attack increases, lift

00:22:41.599 --> 00:22:42.633
initially increases.

00:22:43.233 --> 00:22:44.566
This is how aircraft climb.

00:22:45.133 --> 00:22:46.933
However, there's a critical angle at

00:22:46.933 --> 00:22:48.033
which the airflow can no

00:22:48.033 --> 00:22:49.200
longer follow the contour

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:51.966
of the wing and it separates, causing a

00:22:51.966 --> 00:22:53.099
dramatic loss of lift.

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:56.333
This is a stall and it can happen at any

00:22:56.333 --> 00:22:57.333
speed if the critical

00:22:57.333 --> 00:22:58.766
angle of attack is exceeded.

00:23:00.799 --> 00:23:03.233
In cruise flight at high altitude, the air is

00:23:03.233 --> 00:23:04.900
thinner, providing less lift.

00:23:05.466 --> 00:23:07.166
The margin between normal cruise speed

00:23:07.166 --> 00:23:08.733
and stall speed is narrower,

00:23:09.333 --> 00:23:10.500
known as the "coffin corner."

00:23:11.333 --> 00:23:12.599
And recovery requires more

00:23:12.599 --> 00:23:14.133
altitude to regain airspeed.

00:23:15.766 --> 00:23:18.033
Most pilot training before this incident focused

00:23:18.033 --> 00:23:21.000
on stall recognition and recovery at a

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:22.233
relatively low altitude,

00:23:22.833 --> 00:23:24.133
not at cruise altitude.

00:23:25.766 --> 00:23:28.200
Over blaring alarms and alerts, Robert

00:23:28.200 --> 00:23:30.533
confusingly asks, "But we've got the

00:23:30.533 --> 00:23:32.500
engines, what's happening? Do you

00:23:32.500 --> 00:23:33.933
understand what's happening or not?"

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:37.000
Bonin doesn't answer for seven seconds

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:39.599
until he finally admits, "I don't have

00:23:39.599 --> 00:23:41.799
control of the airplane anymore now,"

00:23:41.799 --> 00:23:43.000
followed by, "I don't have

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:44.400
control of the airplane at all."

00:23:45.466 --> 00:23:47.533
Approximately 90 seconds have now elapsed

00:23:47.533 --> 00:23:48.766
since this unfortunate

00:23:48.766 --> 00:23:50.266
sequence of events began.

00:23:51.133 --> 00:23:53.099
Bonin's response, pulling back on the

00:23:53.099 --> 00:23:54.833
sidestick when faced with unreliable

00:23:54.833 --> 00:23:57.966
airspeed, might seem puzzling, but human

00:23:57.966 --> 00:23:59.766
factors help explain this reaction.

00:24:01.566 --> 00:24:03.533
The startle effect is a physiological

00:24:03.533 --> 00:24:05.666
response to unexpected stimuli.

00:24:06.599 --> 00:24:08.166
When suddenly confronted with multiple

00:24:08.166 --> 00:24:11.299
alarms, unusual aircraft behavior, and

00:24:11.299 --> 00:24:12.333
confusing instrument

00:24:12.433 --> 00:24:15.033
readings, pilots can experience increased

00:24:15.033 --> 00:24:16.400
heart rate, muscle

00:24:16.400 --> 00:24:18.366
tension, narrowed perceptual

00:24:18.633 --> 00:24:20.933
field, tunnel vision, and difficulty

00:24:20.933 --> 00:24:22.633
processing complex information.

00:24:23.366 --> 00:24:24.599
Hearing is often one of the

00:24:24.599 --> 00:24:26.200
first senses to deteriorate.

00:24:27.099 --> 00:24:28.433
They also tend to revert to more

00:24:28.433 --> 00:24:29.799
familiar, ingrained

00:24:29.799 --> 00:24:32.299
responses, and that seems to be

00:24:32.599 --> 00:24:33.299
what might have been

00:24:33.299 --> 00:24:34.500
happening to Bonin here.

00:24:35.866 --> 00:24:37.866
When Airbus aircraft are in Normal Law,

00:24:38.566 --> 00:24:39.500
as they usually are,

00:24:40.033 --> 00:24:41.333
the standard response to

00:24:41.333 --> 00:24:43.333
unexpected turbulence or discomfort at

00:24:43.333 --> 00:24:45.000
cruise is often to climb

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:46.633
slightly for a smoother ride.

00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:49.799
Bonin's instinctive pullback response may

00:24:49.799 --> 00:24:51.833
have been this type of habitual reaction,

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:54.233
inappropriate for the actual situation

00:24:54.233 --> 00:24:55.633
but perhaps triggered

00:24:55.633 --> 00:24:57.033
by stress and confusion.

00:24:58.799 --> 00:25:00.266
Cognitive tunneling further

00:25:00.266 --> 00:25:01.433
compounded the problem.

00:25:02.166 --> 00:25:04.299
This psychological phenomenon causes

00:25:04.299 --> 00:25:06.099
people under stress to

00:25:06.099 --> 00:25:07.733
fixate on certain information

00:25:08.133 --> 00:25:09.799
while missing other critical cues.

00:25:10.799 --> 00:25:12.733
The pilots became focused on controlling

00:25:12.733 --> 00:25:14.400
the aircraft's roll and

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:15.299
managing the immediate

00:25:15.333 --> 00:25:16.866
crisis of lost airspeed

00:25:17.299 --> 00:25:18.900
while missing the developing stall

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:20.900
condition despite physical

00:25:20.900 --> 00:25:23.333
cues like airframe buffeting and the

00:25:23.333 --> 00:25:24.466
stall warning sound.

00:25:25.733 --> 00:25:27.833
Once Bonin admitted that he no longer had

00:25:27.833 --> 00:25:28.933
control of the airplane.

00:25:29.533 --> 00:25:31.833
It took Robert an agonizing three

00:25:31.833 --> 00:25:33.666
seconds to request "controls

00:25:33.666 --> 00:25:34.833
to the left" as he attempted

00:25:34.833 --> 00:25:35.866
to take control.

00:25:36.933 --> 00:25:38.566
The proper procedure for transferring

00:25:38.566 --> 00:25:40.633
control requires a formal

00:25:40.633 --> 00:25:42.433
"taking control" call-out

00:25:42.933 --> 00:25:44.433
and pressing the priority button.

00:25:45.333 --> 00:25:46.200
Neither pilot follows

00:25:46.200 --> 00:25:47.533
this procedure completely.

00:25:49.166 --> 00:25:50.966
To add to the already heightened level of

00:25:50.966 --> 00:25:52.133
confusion in the cockpit,

00:25:52.500 --> 00:25:53.633
without saying anything,

00:25:54.266 --> 00:25:56.133
Bonin almost immediately takes back

00:25:56.133 --> 00:25:57.533
control again and

00:25:57.533 --> 00:25:59.133
continues his nose-up inputs.

00:26:00.299 --> 00:26:01.366
A few seconds later,

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:03.866
Robert exclaims "What is that?"

00:26:04.033 --> 00:26:05.466
still struggling to understand the

00:26:05.466 --> 00:26:06.466
aircraft's behavior.

00:26:06.466 --> 00:26:06.933
"I have the

00:26:06.933 --> 00:26:08.266
impression we have, the speed,"

00:26:08.633 --> 00:26:09.233
Bonin responds.

00:26:11.099 --> 00:26:12.566
With the aircraft now in a fully

00:26:12.566 --> 00:26:14.866
developed stall, its behavior became

00:26:14.866 --> 00:26:15.966
increasingly difficult

00:26:15.966 --> 00:26:16.533
to manage.

00:26:18.299 --> 00:26:20.666
At this point, Captain Dubois returns to

00:26:20.666 --> 00:26:21.766
the cockpit and

00:26:21.766 --> 00:26:23.466
immediately asks "Hey, what are

00:26:23.533 --> 00:26:24.266
you doing?" as he

00:26:24.266 --> 00:26:25.933
observes the chaotic situation.

00:26:26.833 --> 00:26:28.700
It might seem like it took a considerable

00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:30.833
length of time for the Captain to show up

00:26:31.333 --> 00:26:33.866
and probably felt like forever to Robert,

00:26:34.233 --> 00:26:35.700
who was the one calling him.

00:26:36.833 --> 00:26:39.033
But in reality, it was only a little over

00:26:39.033 --> 00:26:40.466
90 seconds since the

00:26:40.466 --> 00:26:41.799
autopilot got disconnected

00:26:42.366 --> 00:26:44.433
and less than one minute from when Robert

00:26:44.433 --> 00:26:45.966
started pushing the call button.

00:26:46.833 --> 00:26:47.533
Robert:

00:26:47.533 --> 00:26:48.000
"What's happening?

00:26:48.366 --> 00:26:49.299
I don't know, I don't

00:26:49.299 --> 00:26:50.066
know what's happening."

00:26:50.066 --> 00:26:50.966
Bonin: "We're losing

00:26:50.966 --> 00:26:52.366
control of the airplane there."

00:26:52.533 --> 00:26:53.866
Robert: "We lost all

00:26:53.866 --> 00:26:54.933
control of the airplane.

00:26:55.233 --> 00:26:56.433
We don't understand anything.

00:26:56.433 --> 00:26:57.166
We've tried everything."

00:26:57.733 --> 00:26:59.866
By this time, all recorded speeds have

00:26:59.866 --> 00:27:02.033
become invalid again, and the stall

00:27:02.033 --> 00:27:02.933
warning has stopped,

00:27:03.366 --> 00:27:05.033
not because the stall condition has been

00:27:05.033 --> 00:27:07.133
resolved, but because the system

00:27:07.133 --> 00:27:08.366
invalidates the angle

00:27:08.366 --> 00:27:10.933
of attack values when measured airspeed

00:27:10.933 --> 00:27:12.466
falls below 60 knots.

00:27:13.966 --> 00:27:16.433
The aircraft is now descending rapidly at

00:27:16.433 --> 00:27:18.299
a staggering 10,000 feet per minute.

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:21.166
The angle of attack exceeds 40 degrees,

00:27:21.633 --> 00:27:23.433
well beyond the critical stall angle.

00:27:24.866 --> 00:27:27.799
The engines are at nearly 100%, producing

00:27:27.799 --> 00:27:29.666
maximum thrust, yet the

00:27:29.666 --> 00:27:30.633
aircraft is descending

00:27:30.733 --> 00:27:32.299
due to the extreme angle of attack,

00:27:32.666 --> 00:27:33.966
creating overwhelming drag.

00:27:35.299 --> 00:27:37.133
The aircraft develops roll oscillations

00:27:37.133 --> 00:27:38.566
that reach up to 40 degrees.

00:27:40.133 --> 00:27:41.866
Completely disoriented, Bonin holds his

00:27:41.866 --> 00:27:42.799
sidestick at the left

00:27:42.799 --> 00:27:44.266
and full nose-up stop for

00:27:44.333 --> 00:27:47.233
about 30 seconds, inputs that only worsen

00:27:47.233 --> 00:27:48.266
the stalled condition.

00:27:49.233 --> 00:27:51.099
What follows is a few more seconds of

00:27:51.099 --> 00:27:52.833
confusion as the pilots try

00:27:52.833 --> 00:27:54.333
to make sense of the situation.

00:27:55.066 --> 00:27:56.200
Bonin: "I have a problem.

00:27:56.433 --> 00:27:57.133
It's that I don't have

00:27:57.133 --> 00:27:57.966
vertical speed indication."

00:27:58.200 --> 00:27:59.266
Dubois: "Alright."

00:27:59.599 --> 00:28:01.400
Bonin: "We have no more valid displays."

00:28:01.733 --> 00:28:03.333
Robert: "I have no more displays."

00:28:04.333 --> 00:28:06.033
At this moment, the thrust levers are at

00:28:06.033 --> 00:28:07.233
idle, though the

00:28:07.233 --> 00:28:09.433
engines' N1 remains at 55%.

00:28:11.166 --> 00:28:13.099
It's plausible that Bonin now considers

00:28:13.099 --> 00:28:14.266
that their aircraft might

00:28:14.266 --> 00:28:15.533
be at risk of over-speed

00:28:15.833 --> 00:28:17.766
when he repeats his earlier observation:

00:28:18.166 --> 00:28:18.766
"I have the impression

00:28:18.766 --> 00:28:20.033
that we have some crazy speed.

00:28:20.433 --> 00:28:21.166
No what do you think?"

00:28:21.633 --> 00:28:23.033
Perhaps he reached out for the speed

00:28:23.033 --> 00:28:23.866
brakes after saying

00:28:23.866 --> 00:28:25.633
that, because Robert quickly

00:28:25.866 --> 00:28:27.733
warns, "No, above all, don't extend,"

00:28:28.099 --> 00:28:29.833
followed again by, "Don't extend."

00:28:30.333 --> 00:28:32.133
Bonin then makes pitch-down inputs,

00:28:32.433 --> 00:28:32.733
momentarily

00:28:32.733 --> 00:28:34.366
decreasing the angle of attack.

00:28:35.133 --> 00:28:37.033
The stall warning briefly reactivates

00:28:37.033 --> 00:28:38.966
with "Stall, stall" as

00:28:38.966 --> 00:28:39.866
the airspeed momentarily

00:28:40.133 --> 00:28:42.633
increases above 60 knots due to Bonin's

00:28:42.633 --> 00:28:44.233
slight forward sidestick movement.

00:28:45.599 --> 00:28:47.400
Whenever back pressure was maintained on

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:48.900
the sidestick, the angle

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:50.366
of attack remained high,

00:28:50.866 --> 00:28:52.966
but the airspeed was so low that the

00:28:52.966 --> 00:28:54.366
stall warning was silent.

00:28:56.033 --> 00:28:57.933
Because the Airbus stall warning system

00:28:57.933 --> 00:28:59.366
was designed to deactivate

00:28:59.366 --> 00:29:01.166
at extremely low airspeed

00:29:01.533 --> 00:29:02.966
when angle of attack data

00:29:02.966 --> 00:29:04.099
is considered unreliable.

00:29:05.299 --> 00:29:07.366
When they momentarily pushed forward the

00:29:07.366 --> 00:29:08.633
correct recovery action,

00:29:09.066 --> 00:29:10.799
the airspeed would begin to increase just

00:29:10.799 --> 00:29:11.900
enough to reactivate

00:29:11.900 --> 00:29:13.066
the stall warning system.

00:29:14.466 --> 00:29:15.900
This created the counterintuitive

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:17.599
impression that pushing

00:29:17.599 --> 00:29:19.133
forward was causing the warning

00:29:19.533 --> 00:29:21.533
while pulling back was silencing it.

00:29:22.733 --> 00:29:23.299
In this unusual situation of a deep stall at

00:29:23.299 --> 00:29:25.166
In this unusual situation of a deep stall at

00:29:25.166 --> 00:29:26.633
high altitude, the

00:29:26.633 --> 00:29:28.166
system essentially warned them

00:29:28.599 --> 00:29:30.666
when they were doing the right thing and

00:29:30.666 --> 00:29:32.033
stopped warning them when

00:29:32.033 --> 00:29:32.966
they were doing the wrong

00:29:32.966 --> 00:29:35.633
thing—an incredibly confusing situation

00:29:35.633 --> 00:29:37.166
for the pilots in an

00:29:37.166 --> 00:29:38.866
already high-stress environment.

00:29:39.766 --> 00:29:41.366
Bonin then announces "so we're still

00:29:41.366 --> 00:29:42.599
going down," finally

00:29:42.599 --> 00:29:44.299
recognizing their massive descent

00:29:44.333 --> 00:29:44.766
rate.

00:29:45.500 --> 00:29:47.633
Robert responds "we're pulling" as they

00:29:47.633 --> 00:29:48.866
attempt to stop the descent.

00:29:50.266 --> 00:29:52.933
A second later Robert astonishingly asks

00:29:52.933 --> 00:29:54.233
"What do you think about it?

00:29:54.466 --> 00:29:55.000
What do you think?

00:29:55.266 --> 00:29:56.333
What do we need to do?"

00:29:56.333 --> 00:29:58.200
showing how completely the situational

00:29:58.200 --> 00:29:59.966
awareness has broken down.

00:30:00.566 --> 00:30:02.566
The captain, clearly just as confused,

00:30:02.566 --> 00:30:04.166
responds "There, I don't

00:30:04.166 --> 00:30:05.333
know, there, it's going down."

00:30:05.766 --> 00:30:07.866
5 seconds later Bonin says "That's good,

00:30:07.866 --> 00:30:08.366
we should be wings

00:30:08.366 --> 00:30:09.833
level, no it won't," showing

00:30:09.866 --> 00:30:11.833
concern about the aircraft's bank angle.

00:30:12.700 --> 00:30:14.666
The captain advises "The wings to flat

00:30:14.666 --> 00:30:15.533
horizon, the standby

00:30:15.533 --> 00:30:17.200
horizon," trying to get Bonin to

00:30:17.233 --> 00:30:18.533
use their backup attitude

00:30:18.533 --> 00:30:20.666
indicator to level the wings.

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:23.900
Almost two and a half minutes into their

00:30:23.900 --> 00:30:25.266
ordeal, they've passed

00:30:25.266 --> 00:30:26.933
through Flight Level 250,

00:30:27.666 --> 00:30:29.000
25,000 feet

00:30:29.400 --> 00:30:32.200
having lost over 13,000 feet of altitude

00:30:32.599 --> 00:30:35.533
 in less than a minute with their vertical speed

00:30:35.533 --> 00:30:38.000
indicating more than 15,000 feet per

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:39.299
minute descent at times.

00:30:40.466 --> 00:30:42.900
The confusion continues with conflicting

00:30:42.900 --> 00:30:45.166
perceptions of the aircraft's attitude.

00:30:45.766 --> 00:30:47.333
Robert: "You're climbing," followed

00:30:47.333 --> 00:30:48.599
immediately by "You're

00:30:48.599 --> 00:30:50.299
going down, down, down." Bonin:

00:30:50.299 --> 00:30:52.599
"Am I going down now?" to which Robert and

00:30:52.599 --> 00:30:54.433
Dubois respond with conflicting answers.

00:30:55.299 --> 00:30:57.866
Robert: "Go down." Dubois:  

00:30:57.866 --> 00:30:59.933
"No you climb there. You’re climbing!”

00:30:59.933 --> 00:31:02.433
Bonin: "I'm climbing okay so we're going down"

00:31:02.866 --> 00:31:04.666
More stall warnings, the cricket chime and

00:31:04.666 --> 00:31:05.833
the continuous C-chord

00:31:05.833 --> 00:31:08.533
alert. Bonin: "Okay, we're in TOGA."

00:31:09.099 --> 00:31:10.799
Referring to the thrust setting.

00:31:10.799 --> 00:31:12.133
"what are we here?" followed by

00:31:12.133 --> 00:31:14.900
 "On alti what do we have here?" and repeating that

00:31:14.900 --> 00:31:17.233
same question seconds later, showing that

00:31:17.233 --> 00:31:18.233
he's trying hard to

00:31:18.233 --> 00:31:19.366
determine their altitude.

00:31:19.933 --> 00:31:21.633
In apparent disbelief, the

00:31:21.633 --> 00:31:23.299
captain utters: "It's impossible."

00:31:23.799 --> 00:31:26.333
Robert asks, "What do you mean on altitude?"

00:31:26.333 --> 00:31:29.233
Bonin: "Yeah? Yeah. Yeah. I'm going down, no"

00:31:29.333 --> 00:31:32.066
Robert: "You're going down, yes" Dubois:

00:31:32.066 --> 00:31:33.933
"Hey, you, you're in..." but

00:31:33.933 --> 00:31:35.133
doesn't complete the thought.

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:37.933
Dubois: "Get the wings horizontal." Robert,

00:31:37.933 --> 00:31:39.933
echoing Dubois: "Get the wings horizontal."

00:31:39.933 --> 00:31:42.033
Bonin: "That's what I'm trying to do."

00:31:42.666 --> 00:31:44.200
Dubois repeats himself: "Get

00:31:44.200 --> 00:31:46.000
the wings horizontal." Bonin:

00:31:46.233 --> 00:31:48.533
"I'm at the limit with the roll." The

00:31:48.533 --> 00:31:51.299
dual input warning then sounds, indicating

00:31:51.299 --> 00:31:53.733
that both Bonin and Robert are making

00:31:54.033 --> 00:31:56.233
simultaneous inputs on their sidesticks.

00:31:57.233 --> 00:31:59.200
The captain mentions the rudder bar,

00:31:59.200 --> 00:32:01.099
suggesting use of rudder input to help

00:32:01.099 --> 00:32:02.500
control the aircraft: "Wings

00:32:02.500 --> 00:32:05.133
horizontal, go gently, gently."

00:32:06.599 --> 00:32:09.299
Even more frustration follows as Robert

00:32:09.299 --> 00:32:11.200
declares: "We lost it all at left

00:32:11.333 --> 00:32:13.933
I've got nothing there," possibly referring

00:32:13.933 --> 00:32:16.599
to loss of control authority. Bonin

00:32:16.599 --> 00:32:18.233
remarks: "We're there. We're there.

00:32:18.233 --> 00:32:19.033
We're passing level

00:32:19.033 --> 00:32:20.733
100," meaning 10,000 feet.

00:32:22.099 --> 00:32:23.700
The crew has now been struggling with the

00:32:23.700 --> 00:32:25.633
situation for just over three minutes,

00:32:25.633 --> 00:32:28.133
with multiple control transfers and

00:32:28.133 --> 00:32:30.033
conflicting inputs throughout the descent.

00:32:31.566 --> 00:32:34.333
Finally, Robert says: "Wait, me, I have, I

00:32:34.333 --> 00:32:35.200
have the controls eh."

00:32:35.500 --> 00:32:37.766
Another dual input announcement showing

00:32:37.766 --> 00:32:39.900
that two pilots are again attempting to

00:32:39.900 --> 00:32:42.299
control the aircraft at the same time.

00:32:42.633 --> 00:32:44.533
Bonin: "What is... how come we're continuing

00:32:44.533 --> 00:32:46.633
to go right down now?" Robert: "Try to find

00:32:46.633 --> 00:32:47.366
what you can do with

00:32:47.366 --> 00:32:48.233
your controls up there,

00:32:48.299 --> 00:32:50.533
the primaries and so on," presumably

00:32:50.533 --> 00:32:53.733
referring to primary flight controls. At

00:32:53.733 --> 00:32:54.599
this point the

00:32:54.599 --> 00:32:56.166
captain responds: "Do anything."

00:32:56.233 --> 00:32:58.433
Followed by "It won't do anything,"

00:32:58.766 --> 00:33:00.466
indicating his growing desperation,

00:33:00.933 --> 00:33:02.733
realizing that his co-pilots' control

00:33:02.733 --> 00:33:04.900
inputs are no longer effective.

00:33:05.333 --> 00:33:06.466
Robert calls out

00:33:06.833 --> 00:33:07.933
"9,000 feet" as they

00:33:07.933 --> 00:33:09.700
continue with their rapid descent.

00:33:10.333 --> 00:33:11.966
Dubois: "Careful with the rudder bar there,"

00:33:11.966 --> 00:33:13.033
again warning about

00:33:13.033 --> 00:33:14.200
excessive rudder inputs.

00:33:14.833 --> 00:33:16.933
Robert: "Climb, climb, climb, climb."

00:33:17.400 --> 00:33:19.133
Apparently still not recognizing the

00:33:19.133 --> 00:33:20.200
stall condition they were

00:33:20.200 --> 00:33:22.066
faced with. Bonin responds:

00:33:22.133 --> 00:33:24.166
"But I've been at maximum nose up for a while"

00:33:24.166 --> 00:33:25.366
revealing he's been maintaining

00:33:25.366 --> 00:33:26.733
the worst possible input

00:33:26.733 --> 00:33:27.966
during a stall situation.

00:33:28.599 --> 00:33:30.799
Another dual input warning sounds as Dubois

00:33:30.799 --> 00:33:32.599
says "No, no, no, don't climb!"

00:33:32.599 --> 00:33:34.133
Robert: "So go down"

00:33:34.166 --> 00:33:36.599
Followed by, "So give me the controls, the

00:33:36.599 --> 00:33:38.799
controls to me, controls to me," trying to

00:33:38.799 --> 00:33:40.666
take definitive control of the aircraft.

00:33:40.733 --> 00:33:45.200
Bonin at last says "Go ahead, you have the

00:33:45.200 --> 00:33:46.333
controls. We are still in TOGA,"

00:33:46.900 --> 00:33:49.200
formally transferring control to Robert.

00:33:49.200 --> 00:33:53.099
Dubois: "So wait, A.P. off." Dubois: "Watch

00:33:53.099 --> 00:33:54.366
out, you're pitching up there."

00:33:54.799 --> 00:33:56.933
Robert: "I'm pitching up." Dubois:

00:33:56.933 --> 00:33:59.533
"You're pitching up." Robert:

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:03.133
"I'm pitching up." Bonin: "Well, we need to... we

00:34:03.133 --> 00:34:04.366
are at 4,000 feet." Dubois:

00:34:04.366 --> 00:34:05.700
"You're pitching up"

00:34:05.700 --> 00:34:07.833
The Ground Proximity Warning System

00:34:07.833 --> 00:34:10.733
urgently announces "Sink rate, sink rate"

00:34:10.733 --> 00:34:12.033
and then "Pull up" warnings.

00:34:12.333 --> 00:34:14.766
Dubois: "Go on, pull." Bonin:

00:34:14.766 --> 00:34:16.599
"Let's go, pull up, pull up, pull up."

00:34:16.966 --> 00:34:18.433
Bonin: "We're going to crash.

00:34:18.766 --> 00:34:21.033
This can't be true. But what's happening?"

00:34:21.033 --> 00:34:23.599
Dubois: "10 degrees pitch attitude."

00:34:24.833 --> 00:34:26.233
Those were the last words

00:34:26.233 --> 00:34:27.699
ever uttered by the flight crew.

00:34:28.966 --> 00:34:29.066
At 02:14 a.m. and

00:34:29.066 --> 00:34:31.233
at 02:14 a.m. and

00:34:31.233 --> 00:34:33.533
28 seconds, Air France Flight

00:34:34.033 --> 00:34:36.133
447 strikes the ocean surface at a

00:34:36.133 --> 00:34:38.400
vertical speed of ten thousand nine

00:34:38.400 --> 00:34:39.366
hundred and twelve feet

00:34:39.366 --> 00:34:41.300
per minute, a ground speed of

00:34:41.766 --> 00:34:44.666
107 knots, and a nose-up attitude of

00:34:44.666 --> 00:34:46.366
sixteen point two degrees.

00:34:47.766 --> 00:34:49.300
There were no survivors.

00:34:53.633 --> 00:34:55.933
Before we continue and reveal the tragic

00:34:55.933 --> 00:34:57.900
truths contained within the black boxes

00:34:58.733 --> 00:35:00.366
eventually recovered from the ocean

00:35:00.366 --> 00:35:02.133
depths, let's take a moment

00:35:02.133 --> 00:35:03.599
to pay our respects to the

00:35:03.599 --> 00:35:07.699
228 lives lost and their loved ones.

00:35:20.666 --> 00:35:22.033
In the aftermath of the crash,

00:35:22.833 --> 00:35:24.266
investigators faced a seemingly

00:35:24.900 --> 00:35:25.699
impossible task.

00:35:26.500 --> 00:35:29.266
Air France Flight 447 had vanished into

00:35:29.266 --> 00:35:30.000
one of the deepest

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:31.500
parts of the Atlantic Ocean.

00:35:32.166 --> 00:35:33.000
After three

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:34.833
unsuccessful search campaigns,

00:35:34.966 --> 00:35:38.300
it wasn't until April 2011, almost two

00:35:38.300 --> 00:35:40.433
years later, that the wreckage and flight

00:35:40.433 --> 00:35:42.599
recorders were finally discovered.

00:35:43.699 --> 00:35:46.133
Coincidentally, the earlier "Titanic of the

00:35:46.133 --> 00:35:48.733
skies" metaphor seems more apt, as it was

00:35:48.733 --> 00:35:50.000
Woods Hole Oceanographic

00:35:50.233 --> 00:35:52.566
Institution—the same organization that

00:35:52.566 --> 00:35:54.000
found the actual Titanic—

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:56.199
that led the successful discovery of the

00:35:56.199 --> 00:35:58.900
aircraft wreckage approximately six and a

00:35:58.900 --> 00:36:00.099
half nautical miles

00:36:00.099 --> 00:36:02.199
northeast of the last known position.

00:36:02.766 --> 00:36:04.066
The investigation,

00:36:04.699 --> 00:36:06.800
conducted by France's BEA,

00:36:07.099 --> 00:36:09.000
revealed a sequence that began with a

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:11.033
common occurrence: the temporary

00:36:11.033 --> 00:36:13.633
obstruction of the pitot probes by ice

00:36:13.633 --> 00:36:14.966
crystals known as graupel.

00:36:15.233 --> 00:36:17.033
When the airspeed readings became

00:36:17.033 --> 00:36:18.933
unreliable, the autopilot

00:36:18.933 --> 00:36:20.199
and auto-thrust systems

00:36:20.933 --> 00:36:22.933
automatically disconnected as designed.

00:36:22.933 --> 00:36:25.300
What should have followed was a

00:36:25.300 --> 00:36:26.433
straightforward procedure:

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:29.633
maintain attitude and thrust and wait for

00:36:29.633 --> 00:36:31.833
reliable speed indications to return.

00:36:32.766 --> 00:36:35.599
Instead, the flight data recorder revealed

00:36:35.599 --> 00:36:37.099
that First Officer Bonin

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:39.033
maintained persistent nose-up

00:36:39.033 --> 00:36:40.633
inputs for over three minutes.

00:36:41.033 --> 00:36:43.533
His grip on the sidestick remained firm,

00:36:43.533 --> 00:36:46.266
keeping a nose-high attitude despite the

00:36:46.266 --> 00:36:46.833
aircraft's

00:36:46.833 --> 00:36:48.333
increasingly precarious position.

00:36:48.800 --> 00:36:50.900
The investigation would later determine

00:36:50.900 --> 00:36:53.699
that these sustained inputs became a

00:36:53.699 --> 00:36:55.000
critical factor in

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:56.233
the developing situation.

00:36:57.166 --> 00:36:58.300
Several other factors

00:36:58.300 --> 00:36:59.599
contributed to the crash.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:02.333
First, the startle effect of the

00:37:02.333 --> 00:37:03.599
unexpected autopilot

00:37:03.599 --> 00:37:06.000
disconnection at cruise altitude

00:37:06.333 --> 00:37:07.233
degraded the crew's

00:37:07.233 --> 00:37:09.300
capacity for rational analysis.

00:37:09.733 --> 00:37:12.199
Second, the crew had received insufficient

00:37:12.199 --> 00:37:14.900
training for handling unreliable airspeed

00:37:14.900 --> 00:37:16.733
situations at high altitude,

00:37:16.733 --> 00:37:19.599
where aerodynamic characteristics differ

00:37:19.599 --> 00:37:21.533
significantly from low altitude.

00:37:22.099 --> 00:37:24.333
Third, no clear display

00:37:24.333 --> 00:37:26.666
indicated unreliable airspeed,

00:37:27.133 --> 00:37:29.666
making diagnosis more difficult amid

00:37:29.666 --> 00:37:31.199
multiple ECAM messages.

00:37:32.033 --> 00:37:34.800
Most critically, the crew didn't recognize

00:37:34.800 --> 00:37:36.433
the aircraft's approach to stall

00:37:36.966 --> 00:37:40.099
despite warning sounds and buffeting.

00:37:40.099 --> 00:37:43.166
In the A330's Alternate Law, the stall

00:37:43.166 --> 00:37:44.433
warning is harder to

00:37:44.433 --> 00:37:45.966
interpret at high altitude,

00:37:46.333 --> 00:37:48.566
where the margin between normal cruise

00:37:48.566 --> 00:37:49.533
angle of attack and

00:37:49.533 --> 00:37:51.466
stall angle is much smaller.

00:37:52.300 --> 00:37:54.966
The Air France Flight 447 crash

00:37:54.966 --> 00:37:56.800
revealed critical gaps in pilot

00:37:56.800 --> 00:37:58.900
training and aircraft systems,

00:37:59.300 --> 00:38:01.233
prompting industry-wide reforms.

00:38:01.699 --> 00:38:03.833
The two hundred and twenty-eight souls

00:38:03.833 --> 00:38:05.866
aboard weren't just passengers and crew;

00:38:05.866 --> 00:38:08.000
they were parents, children,

00:38:08.300 --> 00:38:09.900
partners, and friends.

00:38:10.233 --> 00:38:12.533
Their legacy lives on in every procedure

00:38:12.533 --> 00:38:14.599
rewritten and pilot retrained,

00:38:14.866 --> 00:38:16.800
ensuring countless families reunite

00:38:16.800 --> 00:38:18.300
safely at airports worldwide.

